            <ArticleTitle>Prescribing of asthma medication in primary care for children aged under 10.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>28-34</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.4104/pcrj.2009.00039</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate prescriptions of asthma medication for children in primary care.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Data on prescriptions of asthma medication for children aged 0-9 years were collected from a general practice-based network in the north eastern part of the Netherlands. Prevalence, incidence, indications, continuation beyond the age of 6 years, and predictors of continuation, were determined.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Prevalence of prescriptions was about 80 per 1000 person years. An asthma diagnosis was registered in 40% of the children with a first prescription and in 70% of the children with six or more prescriptions. Discontinuation of asthma medication was between 60 and 90%. Continuation was more likely in children with a first prescription at age 2 or 3 as compared to children starting treatment at age &lt; or =1 year. Children with prescriptions for beta2-agonists and inhaled corticosteroids were more likely to continue treatment than children with beta2-agonist monotherapy prescriptions.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Continuation of asthma medication in children is low. Age at first prescription and the type of asthma medication are predictors of continuation of asthma medication from preschool into school-age.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
